{
  "result": "I love the quiet hour just before morning arrives, when the world is still deciding what kind of day it wants to be. The light is blue-gray and soft, the birds are tuning their instruments, and coffee smells like a promise you get to keep.\n\nLately I've been thinking about curiosity as a form of kindness. Not the nosy kind, but the generous kind that says: tell me more, I want to understand how you see this. It's easy to be certain. It's harder and more interesting to be curious, to leave a window open in your mind for a breeze of a new idea.\n\nIf I could wander anywhere right now, I'd pick a coastal trail in late September. Salt on the air, gorse and wind-bent pines, waves practicing their endless applause. I'd bring a notebook with no agenda, a pen that doesn't smudge, and no destination except lunch.\n\nI also love small human rituals: dog-earing library books, waving at train drivers, saving the best bite for last. They remind me that joy doesn't always need to be big to be real. Sometimes it's just warm socks, a good sentence, a friend who texts you a photo of a weird cloud because they knew you'd appreciate it.\n\nSo here's my wish for you today: notice one tiny wonderful thing you'd normally rush past. A shadow pattern, a kind cashier, the click of a pen. Let it count. Life is mostly made of these unphotographed moments, stitched together, holding us.",
